## Solution

Let $Y_i = -\ln(1 - X_i)$. Since $X_i \sim \text{Uniform}(0,1)$, the transformation $Y_i = -\ln(1-X_i)$ gives $Y_i \sim \text{Exponential}(1)$, i.e., with density $f(y) = e^{-y}$ for $y \geq 0$.

**Verification:** For $y \geq 0$,
$$P(Y_i \leq y) = P(-\ln(1-X_i) \leq y) = P(1-X_i \geq e^{-y}) = P(X_i \leq 1 - e^{-y}) = 1 - e^{-y},$$
which is exactly the CDF of an $\text{Exponential}(1)$ random variable.

Therefore each $Y_i$ has
$$\mathbb{E}[Y_i] = 1, \qquad \operatorname{Var}(Y_i) = 1.$$

Let $S_n = Y_1 + Y_2 + \cdots + Y_n$. Then
$$\mathbb{E}[S_n] = n, \qquad \operatorname{Var}(S_n) = n.$$

We want to compute
$$\lim_{n \to \infty} P(S_n \geq n) = \lim_{n \to \infty} P\!\left(\frac{S_n - n}{\sqrt{n}} \geq 0\right).$$

By the **Central Limit Theorem**, since $Y_1, Y_2, \ldots$ are i.i.d. with mean $\mu = 1$ and variance $\sigma^2 = 1$,
$$\frac{S_n - n}{\sqrt{n}} \xrightarrow{d} Z \sim N(0,1).$$

The point $0$ is a continuity point of the standard normal CDF $\Phi$, so
$$\lim_{n \to \infty} P\!\left(\frac{S_n - n}{\sqrt{n}} \geq 0\right) = P(Z \geq 0) = 1 - \Phi(0) = 1 - \tfrac{1}{2} = \tfrac{1}{2}.$$

Therefore,
$$\lim_{n \to \infty} P\!\left(-\ln(1-X_1) - \ln(1-X_2) - \cdots - \ln(1-X_n) \geq n\right) = \boxed{\tfrac{1}{2}}.$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
